---
source: crates/trousseau-cli/tests/help.rs
expression: stdout
---
Save a value under a key

Usage: trousseau set [OPTIONS] <KEY>

Arguments:
  <KEY>  The key to set: a path such as `database/password`. Segments use letters, digits, `.`, `_`
         and `-`, and are separated by `/`. Keys are case-sensitive

Options:
      --from-file <PATH>    Read the value from this file. `-` means stdin, verbatim
      --from-env <NAME>     Read the value from this environment variable
      --binary              Store the value as binary (base64) even if it is valid text. Binary
                            entries are skipped by `run` and `env`
      --env <NAME>          The variable name `run` and `env` use for this entry, instead of the
                            name derived from the key
      --description <TEXT>  A free-text description of the entry
  -h, --help                Print help (see more with '--help')

Global options:
      --store <PATH>            Use this store file. Wins over `--global` and over store discovery
                                [env: TROUSSEAU_STORE=]
      --global                  Use your personal store instead of the nearest project store (the
                                `.trousseau` file in this directory or a parent)
      --identity <PATH>         An identity (private key) file to try when unlocking. Repeatable.
                                Also tried: `TROUSSEAU_IDENTITY_FILE`, the config file's
                                `identity.files`, the default identity file, `~/.ssh/id_ed25519` and
                                `~/.ssh/id_rsa`
      --passphrase-file <PATH>  Read the store's passphrase from this file instead of prompting. One
                                trailing newline is stripped
      --json                    Print one JSON document on stdout instead of text. Errors become a
                                JSON object on stderr
      --quiet                   Suppress informational messages on stderr. Errors still print
      --no-input                Never prompt. A command that would prompt fails instead: exit 4 for
                                a secret, exit 2 for a confirmation

Where the value comes from (first match wins):
  1. --from-file PATH   the file's bytes, verbatim ('-' reads stdin verbatim)
  2. --from-env NAME    the value of that environment variable
  3. a hidden prompt    when stdin is a terminal
  4. stdin              when piped; one trailing newline is stripped

Examples:
  Type the value at a hidden prompt:
    trousseau set database/password

  Pipe the value in:
    printf %s "$TOKEN" | trousseau set api/token

  Store a file, such as a TLS key (binary data is handled for you):
    trousseau set tls/server.key --from-file server.key

  Copy the value of an environment variable:
    trousseau set api/token --from-env API_TOKEN

  Choose the variable name 'run' and 'env' will use, and describe the entry:
    trousseau set db/password --env PGPASSWORD --description "Postgres role"

Notes:
  Setting an existing key replaces its value and keeps its --env and
  --description unless you pass them again.
  To change only --env or --description, use 'trousseau edit'.
